  "worked_solution": "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the **derivative** ${y}^{\u2032}(x)$ is **proportional** to the **slope of the line through $P$ and the origin**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The *slope of the line through $P(x,y)$ and the origin* is given by $\\frac{y}{x}.$\n\n- Since the *derivative* is *proportional to* the slope of the line through $P$ and the origin, we have \n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}(x)=k\\cdot \\frac{y}{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant.\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}(x)=\\frac{ky}{x}.\n$$\n\nIn addition, we're told that ${y}^{\u2032}(x)=-8$ when at the point $(2,-4).$ We can use this to determine the value of the coefficient $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8=\\frac{k(-4)}{2}\\,\u27f9\\,k=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the differential equation describing the curve is\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}(x)=\\frac{4y}{x}.\n$$",
